In Java web applications, one of the most common security flaws arises from the improper use of the `java.sql.Statement` class to execute queries. When user input is concatenated directly into a SQL string, it leads to **SQL Injection (SQLi)**.

The following example demonstrates how an attacker can manipulate a query to bypass authentication or dump database contents.

```java
import java.sql.*;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class VulnerableLogin {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws SQLException {
        // Simulate user input from a web form
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter username: ");
        String username = scanner.nextLine(); // Input: ' OR '1'='1

        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/user_db";
        Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, "root", "password");

        // This code is vulnerable to SQL Injection because it uses string concatenation
        String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '" + username + "' AND password = 'password123'";

        Statement statement = conn.createStatement();
        // The resulting query becomes: SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '' OR '1'='1' AND password = 'password123'
        // This will return the first user in the database regardless of the password.
        ResultSet rs = statement.executeQuery(query);

        if (rs.next()) {
            System.out.println("Login successful!");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Login failed.");
        }
        conn.close();
    }
}
```

